 ### [Streaming and Level-of-Detail (LOD) selection](3087-streaming-and-level-of-detail-lod-selection.md)
-Streaming and Level-of-Detail (LOD) selection is a method for visualizing massive volumetric scenes by adaptively loading data based on viewpoint position, bounding memory usage to visible data and its required "useful" resolution rather than the total scene size. It sets itself apart from static LOD approaches by incrementally updating the data structure to maintain navigation fluidity, avoiding the reload of all data upon viewpoint changes. Unlike [Hierarchical Culling & Multi-resolution Approximation](2010-hierarchical-culling-multi-resolution-approximation.md) which may rely on pre-computed hierarchical structures, this method uses additive ladders to paint a coarse scene from the first chunk and sharpen it as more data arrives, combined with substitutive levels and spatial tiles to reduce the cost of distant or off-screen objects. See [Streaming and Level-of-Detail (LOD) selection](3087-streaming-and-level-of-detail-lod-selection.md).
+Streaming and Level-of-Detail (LOD) selection is a method for visualizing massive volumetric scenes by adaptively loading data based on viewpoint position, bounding memory usage to visible data and its required "useful" resolution rather than the total scene size. It sets itself apart from static LOD approaches by incrementally updating the data structure to maintain navigation fluidity, avoiding the reload of all data upon viewpoint changes.
 
 ### [Focus Point Based Brick LOD Selection](5282-focus-point-based-brick-lod-selection.md)
-A method to determine the appropriate level-of-detail for each brick based on the distance of the brick's center to a user-defined focus point. See [Focus Point Based Brick LOD Selection](5282-focus-point-based-brick-lod-selection.md).
+Focus Point Based Brick LOD Selection is a method to determine the appropriate level-of-detail (LOD) for each brick in a multiscale volume representation based on the distance of the brick's center to a user-defined focus point. It differs from error-driven siblings like [Hierarchical Culling & Multi-resolution Approximation](2010-hierarchical-culling-multi-resolution-approximation.md) because it relies exclusively on geometric proximity to the focus point, ignoring approximation error. This allows for arbitrary texture resolutions per brick, unlike methods with strict texture-to-geometry correspondence.
+
+### [Choosing Alternative Resolutions](5314-choosing-alternative-resolutions.md)
+This method selects a cache-resident resolution level for a volume rendering brick when the desired resolution is not available in memory. It leverages a bitmap of partially mapped resolutions stored in each residency-octree node to efficiently identify candidate levels without random searching. The approach prioritizes lower resolutions before attempting higher ones, iterating through candidates until a mapped page is found or all options are exhausted.
+
+### [Finding Higher Resolution Levels](5315-finding-higher-resolution-levels.md)
+This method determines the next higher resolution level in a residency octree traversal when the current node is only partially mapped at the current resolution. It solves the problem of efficiently skipping resolution levels that are fully unmapped or already processed, ensuring that the renderer accesses the finest available data that still contains partial occupancy information. It is distinct from sibling methods like [VoxelGrid Down Sample](1014-voxelgrid-down-sample.md) which generate data, or [LOD Selection and Culling](5338-lod-selection-and-culling.md) which make final rendering decisions; this method is a specific bitwise lookup operation used during hierarchical traversal to find the next candidate resolution index.
 
 ### [Multi-resolution LOD Volume Rendering with Temporal Coherence](5717-multi-resolution-lod-volume-rendering-with-temporal-coherenc.md)
-A volume rendering technique that assigns different resolution levels to subvolumes based on their importance (projection area, gaze distance) to optimize performance while maintaining visual quality, using temporal coherence to prevent flickering during view changes. See [Multi-resolution LOD Volume Rendering with Temporal Coherence](5717-multi-resolution-lod-volume-rendering-with-temporal-coherenc.md).
+Multi-resolution LOD Volume Rendering with Temporal Coherence is a technique that assigns different resolution levels to subvolumes based on their visual importance (such as projection area, gaze distance, or viewer proximity) to optimize rendering performance while maintaining visual quality. It distinguishes itself from standard LOD methods by explicitly addressing the visual instability caused by frequent resolution changes during view updates, using a temporal coherence mechanism to prevent flickering. Unlike [Hierarchical Culling & Multi-resolution Approximation](2010-hierarchical-culling-multi-resolution-approximation.md) which may rely on strict hierarchical traversal, this method allows arbitrary texture resolutions for subvolumes provided level transitions are constrained.
 
 ### [Face Subsampling](6025-face-subsampling.md)
-See [Face Subsampling](6025-face-subsampling.md).
+Face Subsampling is a Level-of-Detail (LOD) technique for tetrahedral volume rendering that dynamically selects a subset of mesh faces to render, balancing visual fidelity against frame rate constraints. It solves the problem of interactive rendering on large tetrahedral meshes by reducing the number of rasterized primitives without pre-aggregating data into voxels or bricks. It differs from sibling methods like [Hierarchical Culling & Multi-resolution Approximation](2010-hierarchical-culling-multi-resolution-approximation.md) by operating directly on the unstructured mesh topology rather than a hierarchical grid, and from [Focus Point Based Brick LOD Selection](5282-focus-point-based-brick-lod-selection.md) by being primarily face-centric rather than brick-centric.
 
 ### [Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering](6428-flat-blocking-multi-resolution-volume-rendering.md)
-A strategy to load large volume data by dividing the volume into many sub-blocks with equal size but with different resolution or LOD (level of detail) according to the sub-block's importance. See [Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering](6428-flat-blocking-multi-resolution-volume-rendering.md).
+Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering is a strategy for loading and rendering large volume datasets by dividing the volume into sub-blocks of equal physical size but assigning each block a different resolution (Level of Detail, LOD) based on its internal data variation. It solves the problem of memory bandwidth and rendering time for large volumes by avoiding uniform high-resolution storage. Unlike hierarchical structures that change spatial extent, this method keeps block dimensions constant while varying the voxel count within them. It is distinguished from its siblings by its explicit use of a "flat" blocking structure where LOD is determined per block via a variation heuristic, often implemented using min/max octrees.
 
 ### Level-of-Detail Selection
-#### definition
-- An algorithm using an image-based quality metric to select levels of detail for rendering large volumetric data and improving image quality [S1].
+- **Definition:** An algorithm using an image-based quality metric to select levels of detail for rendering large volumetric data and improving image quality [S1].
+
+### LoD Selection via Projected Width
+- **Steps:** Load multiple computed LoD volumes simultaneously (one per LoD level). Choose which volume to lookup for data at each voxel based on the required LoD level. Determine the required LoD level using a metric based on projected width on the camera, following usual PathTracer MipMap selection [S2].
+
+### Pyramid Level Model Selection
+- **Definition:** Determines the level-of-detail (LOD) hierarchy model for streaming volume bricks based on dataset metadata and user policy [S3, unofficial].
+- **Steps:** If policy is 'native', return metadata.pyramid directly. If policy is 'legacy' or unset, return undefined to trigger fallback to a uniform 2:1 virtual pyramid. Fallback occurs when native levels do not fit the native model (e.g., a coarse level is a few voxels short of half its parent) [S3, unofficial].
+- **Parameters:** `pyramid`: 'native' for per-axis factors from metadata, 'legacy' for uniform 2:1 virtual pyramid. Unset enables automatic fallback [S3, unofficial].
+- **Pitfalls:** Native model fails if a coarse level is slightly smaller than half its parent's dimensions; triggers legacy fallback unless explicitly disabled [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of Level-of-Detail (LOD) in volume rendering emerged alongside the need to render large volumetric datasets interactively. Early work focused on generating multiresolution hierarchies. A significant development in LOD *selection* was made by Wang et al., who developed a level-of-detail selection algorithm using an image-based quality metric to improve image quality in GPU-based raycasting pipelines for large volumetric data [S1]. This approach shifted focus from static resolution assignment to dynamic, quality-driven selection. Other related techniques include the generation of high-quality light field representations for novice audiences [S1] and Fourier Domain Volume Rendering which shifts computation to the spatial frequency domain [S1].
+The concept of Level-of-Detail (LOD) in volume rendering has evolved to address the computational complexity of raycasting large datasets. Early work by Fout and Ma proposed GPU-based real-time volume compression and decompression based on block transformation coding [S1]. To improve image quality and performance for large data, Wang et al. developed a level-of-detail selection algorithm using an image-based quality metric [S1]. For interactive access by novice audiences, Rezk-Salama et al. introduced high-quality light field representations integrated into GPU-based raycasting pipelines [S1]. More recent approaches, such as those for microgeometry rendering, utilize multiple computed LoDs loaded simultaneously, selecting data based on projected width metrics similar to path tracer mipmaps [S2]. In web-based out-of-core rendering, pyramid level models are selected based on metadata and user policy, with fallbacks to uniform virtual pyramids when native hierarchical structures are incomplete [S3, unofficial].
 
